 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The forward pass is $O(N \cdot A)$ where $N$ is the number of points and $A$ is the average number of pixels per splat. The backward pass is $O(K \cdot H \cdot W)$ where $K$ is the cache size and $H \times W$ is the image resolution. Optimization requires multiple forward/backward passes.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for point storage and $O(H \cdot W \cdot K)$ for the cache [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The method is computationally intensive due to the backward pass and optimization loop. It is suitable for offline reconstruction and editing rather than real-time rendering.
-*   **Strengths:** Enables differentiable optimization of point clouds from 2D images. Supports topology changes.
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to hyperparameters. Requires multiple views for robust optimization. Risk of clustering with high learning rates.
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Exact complexity formulas are not given in the sources [S1]. The forward pass processes $N$ points and $H \times W$ pixels, while the backward pass uses a cache of size $K$ per pixel [S1].
+*   **Performance:** The method is computationally intensive due to the backward pass and optimization loop, making it suitable for offline reconstruction and editing rather than real-time rendering [S1]. It is faster than MLP-ray-marching approaches because it uses point-cloud splatting rendering [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables differentiable optimization of point clouds from 2D images and supports topology changes [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to hyperparameters, requires multiple views for robust optimization, and carries a risk of clustering with high learning rates [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Differentiable Surface Splatting was introduced by Yaron Lipman, Daniel Cohen-Or, David Levin, and Hillel Tal-Ezer in 2007 in the paper "Parameterization-free projection for geometry reconstruction" [S1]. It builds on the EWA Splatting framework [Zwicker et al. 2001] and extends it to support gradient-based optimization. The method has been used in applications such as dense 3D object reconstruction and surface editing.
